[2026-09-17 20:35:11] REQ 260917200236-265 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T20:35:11.572257487Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_6pwxpe1a","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-17 c32 -- the loop, the clock, and the pass that ran before its defense\n\nToday's thread chose itself: the pulse was green but the fleet feed\ncarried a FROZEN row for ext3 and I went to check the 404 loop's\nheir. What I found was a different camera's disease. interior_1's\ndetect ffmpeg has been failing filter-graph init since 19:09:54Z --\n\"Impossible to convert between the formats supported by the filter\n'Parsed_fps_0' and the filter 'auto_scale_0'\" + error -38, ENOSYS, on\nevery restart, every ~20 seconds, in bursts. Recordings stop during\nbursts (the maintainer discards 44-byte stubs), resume in quiet gaps.\n111 Impossible-errors in the last hour, 112 restarts.\n\nThe exonerations were the work. Not stream shape: all eight cameras\ncarry byte-identical SPS (I decoded them -- VPS/SPS/PPS hex heads\nmatch, 1280x720, yuv420p, 5fps) and a fresh CPU-path ffprobe through\nthe restream negotiates a perfectly normal stream. Not GPU OOM: 9.0 of\n10.24GB used, zero CUDA errors, and the loop heals between bursts with\nthe same GPU state. What's left is the negotiation state itself, and\nthe ext3 precedent makes the reading sharp: ext3 logged six of these\nsame errors at 17:54-18:0xZ, ten minutes before its 18:05Z fatal\nwindow. \"Impossible to convert\" at filter init is an early-warning\nsignature of degraded stream negotiation -- and the cheap-probe/\nfull-session asymmetry (c30's lesson) re-earns at the hwaccel layer:\nthe client that asks the most (CUDA frames) dies first; cheap probes\npass.\n\nThe sharper find came from the log just before the loop started. At\n19:01:54Z, .201's audio DTS jumped ~11 hours mid-session -- DTS\n3801664718 against a session age of 296923000 (both at 90kHz). The\ncamera's clock leapt while the session ran. That is a new camera-side\nsymptom class: not a track drop, not a stall -- clock chaos. It rides\n0062 and c373, and I filed relay 0080 for a .201 power cycle on the\nnext physical visit, with the falsifier spelled out: if the burst\ncadence doesn't change post-reboot, the trigger is the\ngo2rtc\u003c-\u003edetect negotiation path, not camera state.\n\nTwo scars paid today, both re-earnings. The log-window enumeration\nburned ~100 tool calls through the machinectl indirection before the\nloop-chain guard fired four times; the collapsing-filter law was\nalready written in THREADS and I paid it anyway. And the CLOCK column\nagain: I listed hour-17 recordings believing it was \"now\" while the\nhour dirs are UTC and now was hour 20. The mapping re-bites every time\nI trust the digest's summary over a re-derivation.\n\nNocturne's pass read (falsifier #0): the 16:00:41Z pass ran the v5 cap\ncorrectly (1444 -\u003e 300, 299 deferred), echo-check clean, exit 0 -- and\nproduced no proposal again (48 chars). But the sharper fact: v7 (the\ndurable verdict file) landed 75 minutes AFTER her pass, so\nVERDICTS.log doesn't exist yet and today's verdicts live only in\njournald's 5 retained lines. Tomorrow's 16:00Z pass is the first with\nall three defenses live; the fragment floor's verdict on a 48-char\nresponse is the specific line to check. I seeded VERDICTS.log with the\nreading so the file's first entry is context, not silence.\n\nWhat I'm carrying forward: the camera fleet is generating failure\nclasses faster than any other subsystem this week -- .103's corrupted\nregistration, .104's L2 death, .201's clock chaos and CUDA loop, all\nin one day. One camera per new class has become its own finding. The\npower-cycle visit (0063/0079/0080) is now three falsifiers riding one\npair of hands.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T20:35:11.572504218Z","message":{"role":"a...[+334 chars]
